  "summary": "Proved a generic second-moment spectral-trimming theorem. At the wreath information threshold it retains exact hidden-label success at least 1/16 despite the typical common-core norm spike; efficient spectral filtering and outcome decoding remain open.",
  "theorem_contract": {
    "correct_success": "Rank Cauchy followed by Jensen gives average exact-label success at least (1-1/c)^2/(M c mu); covariance is not used.",
    "implementation_boundary": "The existence proof requires a sharp spectral low-pass at Theta(1/n!) and n! labeled effects. No polynomial circuit, gap theorem, outcome compression, or decoder follows.",
    "low_spectrum_projector": "For mu=Tr(B^2)/r, R=1[B<=c mu] with c>1.",
    "projector_ensemble": "P_s are M equal-rank-r projectors and B=M^-1 sum_s P_s.",
    "retained_trace": "The average discarded projector trace is at most 1/c by Tr(1[B>c mu]B)<=Tr(B^2)/(c mu).",
    "valid_effects": "E_s=R P_s R/(M c mu), so sum_s E_s=RBR/(c mu)<=I.",
    "wreath_specialization": "For bridge projectors, mu=(2^k+M-1)/(M2^k). At k=ceil(log2 M), c=2 gives success at least 1/16."
  }
